{"acceptance_length": 1, "acceptance_lengths": [], "accepted_draft_lengths": [], "benchmark": "math500", "completion": "We are given a polynomial:\n\n$$\np(x) = \\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)}\n$$\n\nand we are told that $ a, b, c $ are **distinct real numbers**. We are to find the **degree** of the polynomial $ p(x) $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the structure of the expression\n\nEach term in the sum is of the form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x - x_1)(x - x_2)}{(x_0 - x_1)(x_0 - x_2)}\n$$\n\nwhere $ x_0, x_1, x_2 $ are distinct values. This is a **Lagrange interpolation polynomial** of degree 2, used to interpolate a function at three distinct points.\n\nIn our case, the three terms are:\n\n- $ \\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} $\n- $ \\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} $\n- $ \\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} $\n\nThese are the **Lagrange basis polynomials** for the points $ a, b, c $, and their sum is the **unique quadratic polynomial** that passes through the points $ (a, 1), (b, 1), (c, 1) $, since each term evaluates to 1 at one of the points and 0 at the others.\n\nSo, the sum of these three terms is a **quadratic polynomial** that is equal to 1 at $ x = a, b, c $, and is **identically equal to 1** for all $ x $, because it's a quadratic polynomial that takes the same value (1) at three distinct points.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Conclude the degree\n\nSince the polynomial $ p(x) $ is equal to 1 for all $ x $, it is a **constant polynomial**.\n\nTherefore, the **degree** of the polynomial $ p(x) $ is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{0}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{0}\n$$", "draft_calls": 0, "input_ids": [151644, 872, 198, 10061, 400, 64, 4779, 400, 65, 4779, 323, 400, 66, 3, 387, 12460, 1931, 5109, 13, 220, 7379, 279, 8381, 315, 279, 47311, 198, 78045, 79, 2075, 8, 284, 1124, 37018, 96065, 87, 481, 293, 2376, 87, 481, 272, 9139, 96065, 64, 481, 293, 2376, 64, 481, 272,